Model the orbital‑phase dependence of the X‑ray Stokes Q and U by adding (i) a geometrically thin, relativistic \(\mathrm{Kerr}\) disk (using the standard limb‑darkened intensity and relativistic beaming functions) and (ii) a compact, mildly‑relativistic jet whose synchrotron‐scattered component adds a constant polarization angle.  For each phase \(\phi\) compute the total Stokes vector  

\[
\mathbf{S}(\phi)=\int_{\rm disk} \! I_{\rm d}(\theta,\phi)\,
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos2\psi_{\rm d}\\
\sin2\psi_{\rm d}
\end{pmatrix}
dA
\;+\; f_{\rm jet}\,
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos2\psi_{\rm jet}\\
\sin2\psi_{\rm jet}
\end{pmatrix},
\]

where \(f_{\rm jet}\) is the jet‑to‑disk flux ratio (a free scalar).  Using NumPy/SciPy to integrate over the disk surface for a grid of \(\phi\) yields a predicted polarization fraction \(p(\phi)=\sqrt{Q^2+U^2}/I\).  The test is falsifiable: if the observed \(p(\phi)\) shows a peak at inferior conjunction that exceeds the model’s maximum for any \(0\le f_{\rm jet}\le0.3\), the hypothesis that the jet contributes ≤30 % of the X‑ray flux is ruled out.

